---
title: Overview
summary: SSR islands for SvelteKit. Pages ship as server HTML with no Kit client bootstrap. JavaScript loads only for the components you mark, on a runtime of about 7.6 KB.
related:
  - regions/regions
  - start/quickstart
  - start/install
---

ogygia is an islands architecture for SvelteKit. A page ships as **server HTML with no framework on it**. Only two things load: a small shared runtime, and the code for the components you mark as interactive.

```svelte
<script>
  import Cart from './Cart.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
</script>

<h1>Everything here is static HTML.</h1>
<Cart />   <!-- hydrates only when it scrolls into view -->
```

That is the full idea. Set `csr = false` to remove the Kit client from the route. Then mark the imports that you want interactive. The heading ships zero JS. `Cart` ships its own JS, and only when the page needs it.

::: tip When to use
Use ogygia on content pages that have small interactive parts: marketing, docs, blogs, dashboards, storefronts. Use it when you ship a full framework to run one widget. If your page is one large interactive application, plain SvelteKit with `csr = true` is the simpler tool. ogygia [operates together](/docs/start/install) with those pages, route by route.
:::

The island below is real. It lives on this page with the mark `wake: 'load'`. The text around it stays HTML:

<script>
  import Counter from '$lib/demos/Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <Counter start={10} label="A live island" note="Everything else on this page is static HTML." />
</div>

## Why you want this

- **Ship much less JavaScript.** There is no hydration of the full page. A static page stays static. Most pages cost only the runtime.
- **Write normal Svelte.** Islands are ordinary components. The import attribute is the only new syntax. There is no special island API.
- **It is still SvelteKit.** Remote functions, form actions, the router, and content operate inside islands. There is no forked framework and no patched Kit.
- **One vocabulary.** Client islands, server islands, lakes, and held regions are the same small set of ideas. They are not four unrelated systems.

## How it operates

A normal SvelteKit page starts the Kit client. The client hydrates the full tree. Thus each page carries the framework. ogygia inverts this. You set `csr = false`, and the page is **only HTML**. There is no Kit client and no hydration of the full page.

```ts title=src/routes/+layout.ts
// the whole page opts out of the Kit client
export const csr = false;
```

A **runtime of about 7.6 KB** ships instead. It is a custom element (`<ogygia-region>`) plus an optional router. It waits. JS loads only for the components you mark. The JS attaches to the HTML that is already there. It does not replace that HTML.

## When events occur

You mark an import with two attributes. `wake` says if the component ships client JS, and when the JS runs. `render` says where the HTML comes from, and when. The `wake` schedule is one small set of words:

| Value | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `load` | Immediately |
| `idle` | When the browser becomes idle |
| `visible` | When the component scrolls into view |
| `'(max-width: 600px)'` | When the media query matches |

On an **island**, the word says *when the JS runs*. On a **server island**, it says *when the HTML arrives*. The words are the same. The axes are different. For the full schedule, `visible` margins, and nested regions, see [Timing & nesting](/docs/regions/timing-nesting).

## One primitive, four kinds

Islands, server islands, lakes, and held regions are not four features. They are four settings of one `<Region>` envelope. The canonical table and the two dials are on the [Regions](/docs/regions/regions) page.

## How the docs are organized

The sections build on each other. Read them in this sequence:

1. **[Regions](/docs/regions/regions).** Mark a component, and a part of the page becomes interactive.
   Islands, server islands, lakes, and held regions are four settings of one primitive.
2. **[Data and state](/docs/data-state/remote-functions).** Remote functions give islands a server.
   [Shared state](/docs/data-state/state-context) lets one live object span many islands.
3. **[Content](/docs/content/collections).** A collection changes markdown, JSON, or a CMS into typed
   entries. [`site()`](/docs/content/site) arranges collections into a nav tree, search, emissions,
   and a shell. These docs use it.
4. **[The app layer](/docs/app/spa-router).** The router swaps pages without a reload. It sends all
   server islands in one request. It prerenders the page you will click next.
5. **[Macros](/docs/macros/overview).** `import.meta.og.*` calculates at compile time each value
   that does not change between requests.
6. **[Constraints](/docs/reference/constraints).** The rules that keep all of this correct.

## Start here

- [Install](/docs/start/install): add the package and the plugin.
- [Quickstart](/docs/start/quickstart): your first island, complete.
- [Regions](/docs/regions/regions): the primitive that all marks compile to.

